\section{Durability}{


By default every commit is fully durable: data and metadata are flushed
before \code{\link[=mdbx_txn_commit]{mdbx_txn_commit()}} returns, and a crash at any moment leaves the
database intact. Three flags trade that away, in increasing order of risk:

\describe{
\item{\code{"NOMETASYNC"}}{Skips the metadata flush. A system crash may undo the
last committed transaction. Integrity is never at risk.}
\item{\code{"SAFE_NOSYNC"}}{Flushes nothing on commit. A crash rolls the database
back to the last steady commit — recent transactions are lost, but the
database \strong{cannot be corrupted}. The cost is file growth, because pages
freed since that steady point cannot be reused; \code{\link[=mdbx_env_sync]{mdbx_env_sync()}}
establishes a new one.}
\item{\code{"UTTERLY_NOSYNC"}}{As above, but previous steady commits are wiped
too. A crash shortly after a commit \strong{can corrupt the database beyond
recovery}. 'libmdbx''s own documentation cites a messenger that lost
user data this way. Suitable only for data you are prepared to
regenerate from scratch.}
}

Measure before reaching for these. The cost they remove is per-\emph{commit}, not
per-write: on a benchmark of the vendored library, 2000 single-write
transactions ran 89x faster under \code{"SAFE_NOSYNC"}, while one transaction of
200000 writes ran 1.1x faster. Batching writes into fewer transactions is
usually the same win at no risk.
}

\section{Other flags}{


\code{"WRITEMAP"} maps the database writable and updates it in place, which is
faster but exposes the map to stray writes from the process; it also changes
what \code{"SAFE_NOSYNC"} does, to asynchronous mmap flushes. \code{"LIFORECLAIM"}
reuses the most recently freed pages first, which suits filesystems with
copy-on-write or trim. \code{"NORDAHEAD"} suppresses readahead for databases
larger than RAM, \code{"NOMEMINIT"} skips zero-filling new pages, \code{"EXCLUSIVE"}
takes the environment for this process alone, \code{"ACCEDE"} accepts the flags an
existing environment was opened with rather than conflicting with them, and
\code{"VALIDATION"} turns on expensive internal checking for debugging.
}

\section{What is not here}{


\code{"RDONLY"} and \code{"NOSUBDIR"} are reported but not settable, because they are
the \code{readonly} and \code{subdir} arguments of \code{\link[=mdbx_env_open]{mdbx_env_open()}}. \code{NOSTICKYTHREADS}
is never set: it lifts 'libmdbx''s one-transaction-per-thread rule, which
this package's transaction registry and finalizer ordering rely on.
}

